# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase
#
# Create the project in an EU region — the school operates in France and GDPR
# requires student data to stay in the EU (spec §8).
# Project settings → API gives you all three values.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Safe in the browser bundle. What the anon key can actually do is decided by
# the RLS policies in supabase/migrations/, not by keeping it secret.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# SERVER ONLY. Bypasses RLS entirely. Never prefix it NEXT_PUBLIC_, never
# import it from a Client Component. Used for the Stripe webhook grant, the
# nightly expiry job and minting LiveKit tokens — all after an authorisation
# check has already passed.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Site
#
# The absolute origin, used to build auth redirect URLs. Supabase will only
# redirect to URLs listed in Authentication → URL Configuration, so this value
# and that allow-list have to agree. On Vercel previews, VERCEL_URL is used
# automatically when this is unset.
#
# In production this is https://talibalim.com — no trailing slash, no path.
# It is a NEXT_PUBLIC_ value, so it is baked into the bundle at build time:
# changing it in Vercel does nothing until the project is REDEPLOYED. See
# DOMAIN-SWITCH.md.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare Turnstile — the anti-robot check on the auth forms.
#
# This app draws the widget; Supabase is what enforces it. Both halves must be
# configured, and the order matters: deploy this code with the site key set
# FIRST, then turn CAPTCHA on in Supabase. Turning it on first locks every
# account out, because GoTrue then refuses sign-ups and logins that arrive
# without a token.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY  The widget's site key. Public by necessity:
#                                 the browser renders the widget with it. Unset,
#                                 no widget is drawn and no token is sent —
#                                 which is what local development and CI want.
#
# The SECRET key never comes here. It goes in the Supabase dashboard:
# Authentication -> Attack Protection -> Enable CAPTCHA protection -> Turnstile.
#
# A NEXT_PUBLIC_ value is baked into the bundle at build time: changing it in
# Vercel does nothing until the project is REDEPLOYED.
#
# Cloudflare's test site keys run without a real challenge —
# 1x00000000000000000000AA always passes, 2x00000000000000000000AB always
# blocks, 3x00000000000000000000FF forces the interactive widget. To test the
# real key locally, add localhost to the widget's hostname list in Cloudflare.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled housekeeping.
#
# Vercel Cron calls /api/cron/sweep with `Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>`.
# It cancels checkouts nobody came back to — handing their coupon and pack
# redemption back — and marks expired entitlements expired.
#
# REQUIRED. Without it the endpoint refuses every call, because an open
# endpoint that cancels orders is worse than a job that does not run. Generate
# with: openssl rand -base64 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Prisma.
#
# The Supabase client talks HTTP and needs none of this; Prisma needs a real
# Postgres connection. Both point at the same database.
#
# DATABASE_URL must be the TRANSACTION pooler (port 6543) on Vercel — a
# serverless function cannot hold a connection open between invocations, and
# the direct URL exhausts the database's slots under any real traffic.
#
# DIRECT_URL is the unpooled one, used by migrations and `prisma db pull`: the
# transaction pooler holds no session state, so DDL fails through it.
#
# Both are in Supabase under Settings -> Database -> Connection string.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-1-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1
# DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-1-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PayPal.
#
# Optional. Leave these unset and the school enters its credentials in
# Admin -> Payments instead, which is the easier path but stores a live API
# secret in a database row. Set here, they win over that row — a key in
# Vercel's encrypted store is the safer place for it.
#
# PAYPAL_ENVIRONMENT is 'sandbox' or 'live'. PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID comes from the
# webhook you create in the PayPal developer dashboard, pointed at
# https://your-domain.com/api/paypal/webhook and subscribed to
# PAYMENT.CAPTURE.COMPLETED. Without it, webhooks cannot be verified and are
# refused — which is the correct failure, not a bug.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PAYPAL_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox
# PAYPAL_CLIENT_ID=
# PAYPAL_CLIENT_SECRET=
# PAYPAL_WEBHOOK_ID=
# PAYPAL_CURRENCY=EUR

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional side effects. Each one unset is a logged no-op, never a crash:
# a student still gets access without a receipt, and a missing error sink
# does not take down a page.
#
# Two names that WERE here and are gone:
#
#   RESEND_API_KEY   e-mail goes through the school's own mailbox now, over
#                    SMTP. See the block at the foot of this file. Delete it
#                    from Vercel — nothing reads it.
#   ABLY_API_KEY     from the WordPress plugin this replaced. Delete it, and
#                    revoke the key at ably.com if it was ever set: that plugin
#                    printed it into the HTML of every room.
#
# The classroom runs on LiveKit (above), not Jitsi — this note said otherwise
# for longer than it should have.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SENTRY_DSN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LiveKit — the live classroom
#
# One service for both halves of a class: the audio, video and screen share
# (which travel peer-to-server-to-peer, never through Vercel), and the data
# messages behind chat, raised hands and the whiteboard.
#
# It replaced an embedded third-party room because that room could not tell a
# teacher from a student — the public server will not mint the signed token
# that separates them, so the teacher joined their own class with no controls.
# Here the token is minted by this app, after the database has said who the
# person is and what they may do. A student's token carries no camera source
# until the teacher allows one, and never `roomAdmin`.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LIVEKIT_URL   Config. The wss:// address of the server. Public by
#                           necessity — the browser connects to it — and it
#                           carries no authority on its own.
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY           Secret.
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET        Secret. Signs every token. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# `src/lib/live/server.ts` is `server-only`, so a Client Component importing it
# fails the build rather than shipping the secret to a browser. The WordPress
# plugin this replaces printed its realtime key into the HTML of every room.
#
# Where to point it:
#   * LiveKit Cloud has a free development project — minutes to set up, no
#     server, and enough to test the classroom end to end.
#   * Self-hosted on a free Oracle Cloud ARM instance for a real class. At
#     30–50 students the server relays about 90 Mbps and 40 GB per teaching
#     hour, which fits inside Oracle's free egress with room to spare.
#
# Moving between them is this one URL. No code changes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEXT_PUBLIC_LIVEKIT_URL=
# LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
# LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cloudflare R2 — slide storage.
#
# Slides are PUT straight from the browser to Cloudflare, deliberately: no
# slide is ever streamed through a Vercel function. The app signs a URL for one
# object and hands it over; the bytes never touch our server.
#
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID          Config. The 32-character hex id from the S3 endpoint
#                        (`https://<id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com`) — the id
#                        alone, never the whole URL.
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID       Secret.
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY   Secret. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_.
# R2_BUCKET              Config. The bucket name alone — no URL, no slash.
#
# THE BUCKET NEEDS A CORS POLICY, or every upload fails with a browser error
# that names nothing at all while all four variables look perfectly set. The
# upload is cross-origin by design, so the bucket has to be told our origin:
#
#   R2 → your bucket → Settings → CORS Policy
#
#   [
#     {
#       "AllowedOrigins": ["https://www.talibalim.com", "https://talibalim.com"],
#       "AllowedMethods": ["PUT", "GET"],
#       "AllowedHeaders": ["content-type"],
#       "MaxAgeSeconds": 3600
#     }
#   ]
#
# Both hosts, because both are real: the site is served at www and the bare
# domain 308s to it, and an AllowedOrigins entry matches the browser's Origin
# exactly. List the host the admin pages are actually served from first.
#
# PUT is the upload. GET is the recording: the room loads each slide with
# crossOrigin="anonymous" so the class recorder may draw it. Without GET the
# slide still shows (the room falls back to a plain <img>), but it cannot be
# drawn into the recording — drawing an unreadable image froze the file for the
# rest of the class in October 2026 — so the recording shows an empty frame and
# the teacher sees « Ajoutez GET à la règle CORS du bucket R2 ».
#
# The symptom of a policy missing PUT is "uploads stopped working, the deck
# still shows"; of one missing GET, "slides missing from the recording".
#
# Add a second entry for http://localhost:3000 to upload while developing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
# R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2_BUCKET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Registration when Supabase cannot send email.
#
# Supabase refuses a sign-up outright if it cannot send the confirmation
# message — its built-in sender allows a handful an hour, and a custom SMTP
# that rejects fails the same way. The student sees an error they can do
# nothing about, and the school takes no registrations until it is fixed.
#
# Set this to `true` and the app opens the account anyway: created through the
# service role with the address marked confirmed, then signed in. It is exactly
# what turning "Confirm email" off in Supabase does, available as a variable
# for when that switch cannot be found or changed.
#
# The trade is that an address is accepted without anyone proving they own it.
# Nothing is granted by an address here — access comes from an entitlement, and
# entitlements come from payments — so the cost is a possible junk account, not
# a possible unpaid enrolment.
#
# Leave it unset once SMTP works. Unset is the default and changes nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUTH_ALLOW_UNVERIFIED_SIGNUP=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# E-mail, through the school's own mailbox.
#
# Receipts, contact-form alerts and the welcome message when an account is
# approved. The same mailbox Supabase is pointed at for its confirmation and
# password-reset messages, so there is ONE place to look when a message does
# not arrive rather than two.
#
# From the host's "Secure SSL/TLS" panel:
#
#   SMTP_HOST      mail.talibalim.com
#   SMTP_PORT      465          implicit TLS. 587 if the host prefers STARTTLS.
#   SMTP_USER      contact@talibalim.com
#   SMTP_PASSWORD  the mailbox password        (Secret — never NEXT_PUBLIC_)
#   EMAIL_FROM     Institut Talib Alim <contact@talibalim.com>
#
# Optional. Unset, every send is a logged no-op: a student still gets their
# access, they just do not get a receipt. Losing a side effect must never lose
# the sale.
#
# OFFICE_EMAIL is where the school's own alerts land — a new registration, a
# contact-form message. It defaults to SMTP_USER, which is already the mailbox
# the office watches, so it normally needs no value at all. Set it only if the
# alerts should go somewhere other than the sending mailbox. It is deliberately
# NOT the address printed on the Contact page: that one is for students to
# write to and lives in `src/lib/content/institut.ts`.
#
# SMTP_SERVERNAME — set it ONLY when SMTP_HOST is an IP address.
#
# This exists because a hostname that does not resolve cannot be fixed in code.
# `mail.<domain>` is the name every mail host prints in its panel, and on this
# project it had no DNS record at all: every send died with
# `getaddrinfo EBUSY mail.talibalim.com` while the panel, the password and the
# port were all correct. Dialling the server's IP address sidesteps the missing
# record — but a TLS certificate is issued for a NAME, so the handshake would
# fail next. SMTP_SERVERNAME is the name presented and verified during that
# handshake while the connection goes to the address:
#
#   SMTP_HOST=91.121.51.179
#   SMTP_SERVERNAME=mail.example.org
#
# Certificate checking stays on. Never turn it off to make mail work — that
# trades a broken mail server for one anybody on the path can impersonate.
#
# Unsure which host to use? Admin → Diagnostic → "Tester d'autres serveurs
# d'envoi" tries the configured host, the domain's MX records and the usual
# names, and reports which one accepts these credentials. Whatever it names goes
# here AND in Supabase → Authentication → SMTP, which is pointed at the same
# host and fails the same way.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_HOST=mail.talibalim.com
# SMTP_SERVERNAME=
# SMTP_PORT=465
# SMTP_USER=contact@talibalim.com
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# EMAIL_FROM=Institut Talib Alim <contact@talibalim.com>
# OFFICE_EMAIL=contact@talibalim.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web Push — the notification on the office's phone
#
# A new registration buzzes whatever devices the school has subscribed, from
# Admin → Aperçu → "Activer les notifications". It costs nothing to run: the
# message goes to the browser vendor's own push service, and the VAPID key pair
# below is what proves it came from this site. No account, no third party.
#
# Generate the pair ONCE with `npm run push:keys`. Regenerating it invalidates
# every subscription already stored, and every device has to opt in again.
#
# The public key appears twice on purpose: NEXT_PUBLIC_ is what ships it to the
# browser, which needs it to subscribe. It is public by design — only the
# private key is a credential, and it goes in as a Secret.
#
# Optional. Unset, every push is a logged no-op and the button says so.
#
# iPhone: Safari delivers push only to a site installed on the Home Screen,
# iOS 16.4 or later, and permission must be granted from inside that installed
# app rather than from Safari itself.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:contact@talibalim.com
